Build it inside the Postgres you already have; 38,000 products is far too small to need a separate search service. In `schema.sql`, add a generated `tsvector` column that weights `name` above `description`, and put a GIN index on it:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search tsvector GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
  setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
) STORED;
CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING gin (search);
```

Then add a `q` parameter to the listing route in `routes/products.js`. It would filter with `search @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)` and order by `ts_rank(search, ...)` rather than by `name`.

One decision changes the design: does this need to work while people type, or forgive typos? Full-text search matches whole words, so "headph" or "headfone" finds nothing. If either matters, add a `pg_trgm` index on `name` (the extension is available on RDS) and combine the two. That also affects whether the `'english'` stemmer suits your product names. Rank tuning, highlighting matched text and paging search results come after that decision.
